# Due-diligence review, the way a document grid does it: the target's contracts
# down column A, one field per header, an agent answer in every cell. Headers
# name the answer type (a date, a classification, a currency phrase), so
# formulas and people can both read the column.
# Every answer carries the agent's citation: select a cell for the passage,
# Show Source for the document. Row 2 is a file group: an agreement and its
# amendment read together, later document controlling. Columns J and K are
# conditional: they rate risk from the answers in F, G and I rather than
# from the documents. Select B2:K7 and
# Run Selection; verify (⌘Y) what you've checked, and flag (⇧⌘Y) with a note
# ("deal-breaker", "negotiate", "standard") on what the deal team should see.
